<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pomerania</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Western Pomerania, Swedish Pomerania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pomerania is a historical region on the southern Baltic coast, today divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> occupied the Oder estuary in the 1630s and gained Western Pomerania in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648. Swedish Pomerania gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> a foothold in Germany, though most of it was later lost.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pomerania.jpg" alt="Pomerania" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calle_Dlugie_Pobrzeze,_Gdansk,_Polonia,_2013-05-20,_DD_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Griffin prince, Eric of Pomerania, became king of Denmark, Norway and Sweden in 1397, and later ended his days as a minor duke in <b>Pomerania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The members remained subject to their own lords, from the emperor to the Teutonic Order and the dukes of <b>Pomerania</b>, which limited the league’s capacity to act as a state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick William, the Great Elector (r. 1640–1688), won full sovereignty over it from Poland in the Treaty of Wehlau in 1657 and built a standing army and a central tax administration across scattered domains that stretched from Cleves on the Rhine to <b>Pomerania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the seventeenth century Swedish intervention rescued the Protestant cause in the Thirty Years’ War, and the Peace of Westphalia of 1648 gave Sweden <b>Western Pomerania</b>, Bremen-Verden and Wismar and a voice in the Holy Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In its first centuries Brandenburg was a conquering frontier power, extending its rule over the Slavs east of the Elbe and contending with <b>Pomerania</b>, Poland, the Archbishopric of Magdeburg and Denmark.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…and most of Livonia confirmed at the Truce of Altmark (1629), the mouth of the Oder River in <b>Pomerania</b> in the early 1630s, and the territories formalised in the Peace of Westphalia (1648), which gave Sweden Western Pomerania, the bishoprics of Bremen and Verden and the port of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
